---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="AI governance needs evidence: verifiable AI audit logs — TrustNotch"
  description="AI governance and the EU AI Act require logs of what AI systems do. TrustNotch makes them verifiable evidence: signed, Bitcoin-anchored, checkable offline."
  canonicalPath="/why-it-matters"
>
  <div class="page-wrapper">

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="section hero" aria-labelledby="wim-hero-heading">
      <div class="section__label">AI governance</div>
      <h1 id="wim-hero-heading">
        AI governance needs evidence
      </h1>
      <p class="hero__sub">
        Governance frameworks and the EU AI Act both ask you to keep records of what
        your AI systems do. A record only counts as evidence if you can show it was not
        changed afterwards. TrustNotch makes that provable, by anyone, without trusting
        us.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Article 12 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="article12-heading">
      <div class="section__label">EU AI Act — Article 12</div>
      <h2 id="article12-heading" class="section__heading">The logging obligation</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Article&nbsp;12 of the EU AI Act requires high-risk AI systems to technically
          allow the automatic recording of events (logs) over their whole lifetime. The
          logs must support traceability: spotting situations where the system may present
          a risk or has been substantially modified, feeding the provider's post-market
          monitoring, and letting deployers monitor how the system runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two further articles turn that capability into a duty to keep records. Providers
          (Article&nbsp;19) and deployers (Article&nbsp;26) must keep the logs under their
          control for at least six months, and longer where other EU or national law
          requires it.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the Act does not do is say how to protect those logs from being edited.
          That gap matters more than it looks. A log that someone with admin access can
          quietly change is weak evidence in an audit or a dispute, however complete it
          is. TrustNotch closes the gap: every entry carries a signed receipt and a
          Bitcoin anchor that anyone can check without trusting us.
        </p>
        <p>
          Using TrustNotch does not by itself make a system compliant. That depends on
          what you log, your role under the Act, and your legal context. What it gives
          your logs is the one property the law leaves to you: they can be shown not to
          have changed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout mt-md">
        <p>
          <strong>Dates.</strong> The Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in
          force since 27 July 2026, set when the high-risk obligations apply, including
          Article&nbsp;12. Standalone high-risk systems listed in Annex III, such as hiring,
          credit scoring and education, are covered from 2 December 2027. AI built into
          products covered by Annex I, such as medical devices and machinery, is covered
          from 2 August 2028.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Governance frameworks -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="governance-heading">
      <div class="section__label">Governance frameworks</div>
      <h2 id="governance-heading" class="section__heading">Where logs sit in an AI governance program</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most responsible AI and governance programs rest on a management system:
          policies, risk assessments, clear roles, and records that show the system runs
          as described. ISO/IEC&nbsp;42001, the international standard for AI management
          systems, makes event logging an explicit control. Control A.6.2.8 asks you to
          decide at which stages of an AI system's life cycle event logs are recorded,
          and to keep logging on while the system is in use.
        </p>
        <p>
          Frameworks like this say records must exist. They rarely say how anyone can
          trust them later. That is the layer TrustNotch fills: the audit trail under the
          program, not the program itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          ISO/IEC&nbsp;42001 certifies an organisation's management system, not individual
          tools. No product, TrustNotch included, can make you certified. What TrustNotch
          can do is produce records your auditors can check for themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The trust problem -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="trust-heading">
      <div class="section__label">The problem</div>
      <h2 id="trust-heading" class="section__heading">Why "trust us" logging isn't enough</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most audit-log services store your records in a database they control.
          If a record needs to be shown unmodified, you must trust their assertion
          that nothing has changed — because no independent check exists.
        </p>
        <p>
          In a regulatory audit or legal proceeding, this is a structural weakness.
          An auditor can ask for evidence that logs have not been tampered with after
          the fact — and "we say they haven't" is not evidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same problem applies to AI systems themselves: an agent that can log
          its own actions to a store it influences has a credibility gap. Logs
          submitted to a service that returns signed, externally anchored receipts
          are harder to deny or revise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How TrustNotch addresses it -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="approach-heading">
      <div class="section__label">TrustNotch's approach</div>
      <h2 id="approach-heading" class="section__heading">Cryptographic evidence, not assertions</h2>
      <ol class="steps" role="list">
        <li class="step">
          <div class="step__number" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
          <div class="step__body">
            <h3 class="step__title">Signed receipt at submission</h3>
            <p class="step__desc">
              Every entry gets an Ed25519-signed receipt immediately — a cryptographic
              commitment to the exact content submitted. If the content changes later,
              the receipt no longer verifies.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div class="step__number" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
          <div class="step__body">
            <h3 class="step__title">Bitcoin-anchored batch root</h3>
            <p class="step__desc">
              Entries are batched into an RFC&nbsp;6962 Merkle tree and each batch root
              is anchored to Bitcoin via OpenTimestamps — a public, globally verifiable
              ledger with no single controller. This binds the batch in time without
              relying on TrustNotch's own clock or any centralized timestamping authority.
              Anchoring is asynchronous: Bitcoin confirmation accrues over hours, not seconds.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div class="step__number" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
          <div class="step__body">
            <h3 class="step__title">Verifiable offline, independently</h3>
            <p class="step__desc">
              Any entry can be verified with the open-source
              <a href="/how-verification-works/"><code>trustnotch</code> verifier</a>
              — no API call, no account, no TrustNotch server. The proof is
              self-contained and checkable by anyone, including an auditor using
              their own tools. A proof keeps verifying even if TrustNotch disappears.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- What to log -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="what-to-log-heading">
      <div class="section__label">In practice</div>
      <h2 id="what-to-log-heading" class="section__heading">What to log</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          TrustNotch does not decide what you record. That stays with you, your risk
          assessment and your role under the law. These are the events governance
          reviews tend to ask about first:
        </p>
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li><strong>Decisions:</strong> what the system concluded, with its inputs or a
            hash of them, the model or version, and when.</li>
          <li><strong>Actions and tool calls:</strong> what an agent actually did,
            especially anything that changed data, spent money or contacted a person.</li>
          <li><strong>Human oversight:</strong> approvals, overrides, and the moments a
            person stopped or corrected the system. The AI Act expects high-risk systems
            to support human oversight (Article&nbsp;14), and these records show it
            happened.</li>
          <li><strong>Changes to the system:</strong> model updates, configuration changes
            and new prompts, so later behaviour can be traced to the version that
            produced it.</li>
          <li><strong>Incidents:</strong> errors, refusals, and anything flagged for
            review.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For personal data, <a href="/docs/">send a salted hash</a> rather than the
          content. The proof works the same, and the personal data never reaches us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- GDPR -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="gdpr-heading">
      <div class="section__label">GDPR</div>
      <h2 id="gdpr-heading" class="section__heading">Per-entry erasure without breaking proofs</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Tamper-evident logging and GDPR's right to erasure can seem at odds: if a log
          entry is cryptographically sealed, how can personal data be erased?
        </p>
        <p>
          TrustNotch resolves this with per-entry erasure. You can erase the stored
          payload for any entry — removing it from TrustNotch's servers — while every
          proof for that entry and every sibling entry continues to verify. The Merkle
          tree is constructed over the hashes of content, not the content itself, so
          removing a payload doesn't invalidate any sibling's inclusion path.
        </p>
        <p>
          This means you can satisfy a GDPR erasure request for a specific user's data
          without retroactively undermining the integrity of surrounding log entries.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- MCP angle -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="mcp-angle-heading">
      <div class="section__label">MCP-first</div>
      <h2 id="mcp-angle-heading" class="section__heading">Logging built into the agent's context</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          TrustNotch's MCP server lets AI agents log their own actions directly —
          through the same Model Context Protocol interface they use for everything else.
          The agent calls <code>submit_log</code>, gets back a signed receipt, and can
          later call <code>verify_log</code> to confirm an entry is intact. The whole
          logging loop is in the agent's context, not a separate integration.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because verification is independent of TrustNotch, an agent's logs can be
          audited by anyone — the operator, a regulator, or a third-party auditor —
          using the open-source verifier, without any involvement from TrustNotch.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/trustnotch-mcp/" class="btn btn-secondary">MCP Server →</a>
        <a href="/how-verification-works/" class="btn btn-secondary">How verification works →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Scope -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
      <div class="section__label">Scope</div>
      <h2 id="scope-heading" class="section__heading">What TrustNotch is not</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li><strong>Not a governance platform.</strong> It does not keep your model
            inventory, run risk assessments or manage policies. It makes the records
            those processes produce verifiable.</li>
          <li><strong>Not an observability tool.</strong> It is not built for dashboards,
            traces or debugging. It records the events you choose, so they can stand as
            evidence.</li>
          <li><strong>Not a compliance guarantee.</strong> Using it does not by itself make
            a system compliant with the AI Act or certified under ISO/IEC&nbsp;42001.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="section cta-section" aria-labelledby="cta-heading">
      <div class="section__label">Get started</div>
      <h2 id="cta-heading" class="section__heading">Get started</h2>
      <p class="section__sub">
        Start on the free tier: 10,000 entries a month, the full proof pipeline, no card
        required. Create an account in your browser and issue your own API key from the
        dashboard, or let an agent provision its own free-tier account and key over the
        TrustNotch API. The quickstart walks either path from key to verified proof.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="https://app.trustnotch.com/signup" class="btn btn-primary">
          Start free &rarr;
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/" class="btn btn-secondary">
          Read the quickstart &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Layout>

